    <section id="waterproof-explainer" class="mb-24 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-4">IPX Waterproof Ratings, Decoded</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
          "Waterproof" gets thrown around loosely in grooming marketing. The IPX scale is the actual
          engineering standard — here's what each level means for whether you can shower with your shaver.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl shadow-lg border border-gray-200">
          <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[640px]">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200 text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500">
                <th class="p-5 font-bold">Rating</th>
                <th class="p-5 font-bold">What It Means</th>
                <th class="p-5 font-bold">Shower Safe?</th>
                <th class="p-5 font-bold">Example on This List</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="bg-white divide-y divide-gray-100 text-sm">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-5 font-bold text-gray-900">None / Rinsable</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-gray-600">Heads can be rinsed; the motor body cannot get wet</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-red-600 font-bold">No</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-gray-600">Mangroomer Ultimate Pro, Wahl Total Body Groomer</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-5 font-bold text-gray-900">Washable</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-gray-600">Safe under a running tap, not submersion-rated</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-orange-600 font-bold">Not recommended</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-gray-600">Remington Shortcut Pro (Back Version)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#1E90FF]/[0.03]">
                <td class="p-5 font-bold text-gray-900">IPX7</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-gray-600">Fully submersible in up to 1 meter of water for 30 minutes</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-green-700 font-bold">Yes</td>
                <td class="p-5 text-gray-600">Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000, Hatteker 4-in-1, Panasonic ER-GK60</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-3">
          A device only carries an IPX rating if the manufacturer tested and certified it — "waterproof"
          without a stated IPX number on the packaging usually means rinsable at best.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 border border-gray-100">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">How We Tested Waterproof Claims in Practice</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            To verify these ratings ourselves rather than simply repeating manufacturer claims, we ran
            each shaver under a kitchen faucet for sixty seconds, then let the IPX7-rated units sit fully
            submerged in a bowl of water for the full thirty-minute certified window before drying and
            testing the motor again. All three IPX7 units — the Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000, Hatteker
            4-in-1, and Panasonic ER-GK60 — powered on immediately afterward with no change in motor sound
            or torque, matching their certification exactly. We did not attempt to submerge the
            rinsable-only units, since doing so would void the warranty and risks permanently damaging a
            motor housing that isn't sealed for that kind of exposure. If you're ever unsure whether your
            specific shaver can handle a shower, check the box or manual for an explicit IPX rating rather
            than relying on words like "waterproof" or "water-resistant" alone, since neither term is
            standardized or independently verified the way the IPX scale is.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

