    <section id="buying-guide" class="mt-28 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="text-center mb-14">
          <h2 class="text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-4">Electric Back Shaver Buying Guide</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-lg max-w-2xl mx-auto">The four factors that actually separate a great battery-powered back shaver from a frustrating one.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-10">
          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="bg-[#1E90FF]/10 text-[#1E90FF] w-10 h-10 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-lg">1</span>
              Battery Type &amp; Runtime
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Every shaver in this guide uses a rechargeable lithium-ion (or lithium-based) battery rather than disposable AAs — that's table stakes in 2026. What varies is runtime per charge, ranging from 40 minutes (Remington) to 90 minutes (Hatteker), and how the motor performs as the battery depletes. A shaver with a "flat" torque curve — like the Panasonic ER-GK60 — stays consistent right up to low battery. Others slow down noticeably in the final 10-15 minutes, which matters more if you tend to shave right before the low-battery warning.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">A single back-shaving session typically takes 5-15 minutes, so even a 40-minute runtime covers 2-3 sessions per charge for most men. Don't over-index on the advertised runtime number alone — check whether the manufacturer states charge time too, since a shaver with a shorter runtime but a fast quick-charge feature (like the Panasonic's 8-minute boost) can be more practical day-to-day than one with a long runtime and a slow multi-hour recharge.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="bg-[#1E90FF]/10 text-[#1E90FF] w-10 h-10 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-lg">2</span>
              Motor Torque &amp; Stall Resistance
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">This is the single most overlooked spec in electric shaver marketing, because it's rarely advertised as a number. Torque determines whether the blade keeps spinning at full speed when it hits thick or matted hair, or whether it bogs down, drags, and pulls. In our stall-resistance test — pressing each shaver into a dense synthetic-fiber patch — dual-motor designs with a boost mode (Mangroomer's Power Burst) held RPM the best, while single-motor entry-level units (Hatteker) needed slower, more deliberate passes to avoid stalling.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">If you have thick, coarse, or curly back hair, prioritize a shaver with a stated boost function or dual-motor design over one that simply advertises the longest runtime — a powerful motor that drains the battery faster is usually a better trade than a weak motor that lasts longer but pulls hair.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="bg-[#1E90FF]/10 text-[#1E90FF] w-10 h-10 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-lg">3</span>
              Waterproofing &amp; Shower Use
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">As covered above, only a genuine IPX7 rating guarantees full submersion and shower safety. Three of the six shavers we tested — the Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000, Hatteker 4-in-1, and Panasonic ER-GK60 — carry that certification. The other three (Mangroomer, Remington, Wahl) are rinsable or washable only, meaning you can clean the head under a tap but should never use them in a running shower or submerge the body.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">If shower shaving is a priority for you — many men prefer it because hot water softens hair and opens pores, reducing irritation — narrow your choice to the IPX7-rated three before weighing torque or attachments.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-10">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center gap-3">
              <span class="bg-[#1E90FF]/10 text-[#1E90FF] w-10 h-10 rounded-xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-lg">4</span>
              Attachment Heads &amp; Charging Method
            </h3>
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-4">
              <div>
                <h4 class="font-bold mb-2">Interchangeable Heads</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">More heads mean more versatility, but also more parts to lose and clean. The Hatteker 4-in-1 and Wahl Total Body Groomer offer the deepest attachment ecosystems, useful if you want one motor to also handle chest, arms, and detail work. Dedicated back shavers like the Mangroomer trade breadth for a more purpose-built dual-head design (trimmer + foil) optimized specifically for back hair.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h4 class="font-bold mb-2">USB vs. Proprietary Dock</h4>
                <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">USB-chargeable shavers (Hatteker, Philips Norelco) let you use any USB-A/USB-C cable or power bank as a backup, which matters if you travel or misplace the included cable. Proprietary charging cradles (Mangroomer, Remington, Wahl) are more elegant at home but become a real problem if lost — you can't substitute a generic cable.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">For most men, the practical ranking is: prioritize torque first if you have thick hair, waterproofing second if shower use matters to you, and attachment breadth last — since a dedicated back shaver with fewer heads but better ergonomics for the back usually outperforms a jack-of-all-trades groomer for this specific job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

