            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              How long do electric back shavers actually last on a single charge?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Runtime ranges from about 40 minutes (Remington Shortcut Pro) to 90 minutes (Hatteker
              4-in-1) per charge. Since a full back-shaving session typically takes 5-15 minutes, even
              the shortest-runtime unit handles multiple sessions before needing a recharge.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              What does an IPX7 rating actually mean?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              IPX7 is a certified standard meaning the device can be fully submerged in up to 1 meter of
              water for 30 minutes without damage. Only the <strong>Philips Norelco Bodygroom 5000</strong>,
              <strong>Hatteker 4-in-1</strong>, and <strong>Panasonic ER-GK60</strong> in this guide carry
              that certification. "Rinsable" or "washable" is not the same thing — those units should
              never be used in a running shower.
            </p>
          </details>

