            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Will an electric back shaver stall on thick or curly hair?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              It depends on motor torque, not battery size. In our stall test, the <strong>Mangroomer
              Ultimate Pro's</strong> dual-motor Power Burst mode never lost RPM on dense test patches,
              while lower-torque units like the Hatteker needed slower passes. If you have thick or curly
              back hair, prioritize a documented boost mode or dual-motor design.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              USB charging or a proprietary dock — does it matter?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              USB charging (Hatteker, Philips Norelco) is more convenient because any USB cable or power
              bank works as a backup. Proprietary cradles (Mangroomer, Remington, Wahl) look cleaner at
              home but leave the unit unusable if the cradle is lost, since you can't substitute a
              generic cable.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Is an electric back shaver better than a manual one?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Electric shavers are generally safer for beginners, sensitive skin, moles, and back acne,
              since most use foil or rounded-tip blades that can't nick skin the way exposed manual
              blades can. Manual razors deliver a closer shave and never need charging, but carry a
              higher nick risk until technique is dialed in. See our
              <a href="/best-mens-back-shavers" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">full electric + manual roundup</a>
              for a head-to-head comparison.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              How often should I replace the shaving head or foil?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              Most foil heads should be replaced every 12-18 months of regular use, or sooner if you
              notice pulling or reduced closeness. Rinse after every use and deep-clean weekly to extend
              head life. Wahl's self-sharpening steel blades typically hold their edge longer before
              needing replacement.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
